  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B006",
        "laureates": [
          "Jocelyn Bell Burnell"
        ],
        "citation": "For the discovery of pulsars."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B064"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged from round 1: I still favor recognizing Bell Burnell for the discovery of pulsars. The discovery opened a new observational window on neutron stars, and decades of observations established its lasting importance. I propose one name because the decisive contribution at issue is recognition of the first pulsar signal; the Cambridge team's work remains essential historical context for assessing that credit.",
    "case_against": "Kroll's concern about overlap with the 1974 radio-astronomy prize is serious. The distinction must be grounded in the historical record and a sharply defined citation. The broader team's role also matters: recognizing Bell Burnell's contribution should not imply that she alone built or operated the observing program.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll questions how the pulsar discovery can be distinguished from radio-astronomy work already recognized by a Nobel Prize and emphasizes the wider research effort.",
        "response": "I agree that the earlier prize makes scope review necessary. The proposed citation is confined to the discovery of pulsars, whose subsequent role in neutron-star physics gives it a distinct scientific identity. Whether that distinction is sufficient, and how the Cambridge team's work bears on individual credit, must be settled through historical review."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that Bell Burnell identified and pursued the unusual signals, while the telescope and observing program depended on a broader team whose contributions require archival assessment.",
        "response": "That distinction is central to my proposed credit. The team made the observation possible, while Bell Burnell's recognition of the unusual signals was a decisive discovery contribution. The archival record should establish the respective roles before the committee finalizes attribution."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record supports a sufficiently distinct scope from the work recognized by the 1974 Nobel Prize.",
      "How discovery credit should be allocated among Bell Burnell and the wider Cambridge observing team."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
